  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 139 PM CDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nThursday and Friday...The latest suite of models generally agree on \na not quite as mild a day on Thursday with a 1020+ MB sfc ridge \nsliding acrs the area from the northwest and highs in the upper 60s \nto lower 70s. Several solutions continue to suggest that as the LLVL \nridge departs off to the east, an embedded short wave in steering \nnorthwesterly flow aloft will combine with an increasing LLJ with \nsome THTA-e advection to produce an elevated wing of convergent WAA \ntype showers and even some embedded thunder acrs portions of the MO \nRVR Valley and into central IA Thu night into Friday morning. The \nmodels are still varying on the placement and how far north or south \nthis wing develops, and how far east it can make it through Friday \nmorning battling lingering ridging acrs the southern GRT LKS/OH RVR \nValley. The consensus is that some showers and mid deck of clouds \nwill get into the local area from the west Friday if even in a \ndecaying fashion. But they also show ongoing convergent LLJ \nprocesses enough to keep some showers festering acrs the area for \nmuch of the day until lifting off to the north and east Friday \nevening. The latest ECMWF runs continue to suggest the Friday \nactivity will occur north of I-80 or even Hwy 30. Of course more \nexpected cloud cover and possible precip will keep temps down a bit \nFriday despite an increasing southeast to southerly return flow.\n\nSaturday and Sunday...Digging longer wave upper trof acrs the \nPacific Northwest and ridging amplifying up the GRT LKS will result \nin warming southwesterlies acrs the MID CONUS and toward the GRT \nLKS. Saturday will be a full blown warm sector day with breezy \nsoutherly winds and increasing thicknesses that support highs in \nthe 80s for much of the area. This may be a day of increased \ncropland fire danger risk depending on humidity trends. Then eyes \nturn to the tropical system in the Gulf and the path it may take \ngetting drawn northward inland. How close this system can make it to \nthe local area from late Sunday and into early next week is still \nvery much in question. But definitely something to watch, as the \nlocal area sure does not need the precip off such a system.\n\nMonday and Tuesday...Depending on what or how the above system \nbehaves, early next week otherwise may be characterized by warm upper \nridging and continued above normal temp regime.",
